AmiSurf (formerly AmiStudio Browser) - changes
0.87 (19.10.2026)
  * Fixed the Guru 8000 0004 right after Settings... from the menu button
    (beta report, AmiKit, MUI 5): AmiSurf ran on the small default stack
    Workbench gives a program whose icon has no stack size (MUI 5 asks for at
    least 32 KB), and the 256 KB stack it asks for was never set up. AmiSurf
    now always switches to its own 256 KB stack at start, from Workbench or
    the Shell, all three programs. The log notes stack use at menus, windows
    and quit.
  * Menu button (MUI 5): the chosen item runs on the next pass of the main
    loop, after MUI has fully closed the popup, and the popup is freed only
    when the next one is built; more log breadcrumbs around Settings.
  * Bookmarks bar under the address bar (Firefox style): one button per
    bookmark, one per folder (a popup with its bookmarks), and a » button
    with whatever does not fit. Click opens the bookmark, Shift-click or the
    middle mouse button opens it in a new tab. Turn it on and off with
    Amiga B, View > Bookmarks bar, or the menu button; kept in
    Data/AmiSurf.prefs (BOOKMARKSBAR=ON/OFF, also a tooltype).
  * Menu button > Bookmarks: Bookmark this page, Show/Hide bookmarks bar,
    Manage bookmarks, then your bookmarks with folders as submenus (MUI 5)
    or as their own popup (MUI 3.x and the GadTools build).
  * Bookmark folders: Manage bookmarks > Edit has a Folder field. The
    bookmarks file keeps its format (the folder is an optional fourth
    column). The bar and the list need the 0.87 engine (asb-chromed); with an
    older engine the bar shows a Bookmarks button and a note.
  * Engine: an idle connection to the internal pages server is closed
    without a reply (a reply to it could show up as "forbidden" on the
    next internal page).
  * AmiKit edition: the AmiStudio Host 0.1.7 (amistudio-hostd, unchanged)
    with AmiSurf 0.87's own engine and one portable Chromium in Host/
    (Host/LAYOUT "own", as in 0.85), because the Host 0.1.7 engine is 0.85's
    and has no bookmarks list. Everything still stays in Host/data,
    downloads go to ~/Downloads. After upgrading from 0.86 the old
    Host/amistudio-host/ folder is unused and can be deleted.
0.86 (19.10.2026)
  * Everything stays in the drawer (AmiStudio suite rule): AmiSurf no longer
    writes ENV: or ENVARC:. Settings and the window box are in Data/, the
    AmiStudio identity (ClientId, HostToken, Host) comes from the AmiStudio
    launcher's Prefs/ when it installed AmiSurf, else from Data/. The first
    start moves AmiSurf's own old ENVARC:/ENV: files (prefs, window) into
    the drawer and deletes them; the shared ENVARC:AmiStudio/ClientId,
    HostToken and Host are copied (you stay paired) and left for the
    AmiStudio launcher 0.1.8 to move. ENV:MUI files MUI writes on quit are removed again.
    Settings "Use" now changes only the running AmiSurf.
  * AmiKit edition: bundles the AmiStudio Host 0.1.7 release unchanged (its
    engine has the 0.85 download and new-tab fixes; one Chromium). The Host,
    engine and Chromium keep everything under Host/data, including HOME, the
    XDG folders and TMPDIR (a short /tmp link in deep drawers); downloads
    still go to ~/Downloads. move-to-host.sh --to <AmiStudio>/Host copies
    browser data to the AmiStudio launcher's Host.
0.85 (17.10.2026)
  * Downloads with the AmiStudio Host now show what happens (reported by
    Rich): the status bar says "Downloading NAME to ~/Downloads on the Host
    PC", then "Downloaded NAME (N KB) to ..." or "Download failed or
    canceled: NAME ... - see the Downloads page". The Downloads page (menu
    button > Downloads, Amiga-J) lists them with progress and where they
    went. The files did land in ~/Downloads before, but Chrome only reports
    downloads on its browser connection, which the engine did not have.
  * Links that open a new tab (target=_blank, window.open, "Open link in
    new tab") now show up at once (reported by Rich: only after a restart).
    The engine follows Chrome's tab events and sends the tab list on every
    new, closed or switched tab; a tab the page opened is shown at once. A
    window.open() popup no longer stays a blank tab ("Debugger paused in
    another tab"), and switching back to a tab shows its picture, not the
    last one's.
  * New: single instance and an ARexx port "AMISURF". Starting AmiSurf
    with a URL while it runs opens the URL in a new tab of the running
    copy, brings it to the front and quits. ARexx commands: OPENURL "<url>"
    [NEWTAB], FRONT, GETURL. See docs/AREXX.md (in the source).
  * The download and new-tab fixes are in the engine (asb-chromed): they
    reach you with an AmiStudio Host that has them.
0.84 (17.10.2026)
  * A/V sync on Amiberry: the sound was ~300 ms late (AmiKit, reported by
    Rich). Amiberry buffers sound after Paula/AHI (its own buffers plus the
    SDL device), which the 0.81 calibration in FS-UAE never saw. AmiSurf now
    detects Amiberry (uae.resource host-platform call, or C:host-run) and
    counts 300 ms of output delay after the audio device: the picture is
    held back that much more and less sound is buffered. The log says what
    was found ("av: Amiberry (uae.resource ...), sound output latency after
    the device 300 ms").
  * The time a buffer spends in Paula/AHI is now measured (buffer reply
    times) instead of assumed, and the 0.81 trim of -60 ms is gone: checked
    against the real sound output recorded from FS-UAE (sound within ~0-30
    ms of the picture; with 300 ms of extra output delay simulated, 0.83 had
    the sound ~400 ms late, 0.84 with Amiberry detected ~+25 ms).
  * New: Sound sync. Menu button: "Sound sync (now +N ms) ..." with Delay
    picture +50 / Picture sooner -50 / Reset; MUI menu: "Sound late: delay
    picture +50 ms", "Sound early: picture -50 ms", "Sound sync reset /
    show...". The status bar shows the result, it is saved in
    ENVARC:AmiSurf.prefs (AVSYNC=). AVSYNC=<ms> keeps its meaning (+ =
    picture later) and is added on top of the detected delay: if you set
    AVSYNC=300 for Amiberry before, remove it (it would count twice).
0.83 (17.10.2026)
  * New: pair with the AmiStudio Host any time. MUI menu Project: "Pair with
    Host..." (asks for the 6-digit code from amistudio-hostd pair, replaces
    the token in ENV:/ENVARC:AmiStudio/HostToken, reconnects) and "Forget
    Host token..." (deletes it). The menu button has "AmiStudio Host (pair)
    ...", also in AmiSurf-gadtools: it shows which Host, connected or not,
    where the token is, and offers Pair / Pair again / Forget token.
  * AmiKit edition (AmiSurf-0.83-AmiKit.lha): Host/ now brings the AmiStudio
    Host 0.1.3 (the official release folder, unchanged, in Host/amistudio-host/),
    which includes the engine and the portable Chromium (only one copy),
    so the AmiKit edition uses Host mode like the plain one. Through
    AmiKit's Rabbit Hole AmiSurf starts Host/run-host.sh, which runs the
    Host on 127.0.0.1:9220 (no pairing for the emulator); the Host starts
    the engine. Engine and Host stop after 30 minutes without AmiSurf
    (the Host's own --exit-idle).
    History, bookmarks, passwords and the Chromium profile in Host/data are
    kept. sh Host/run-host.sh --lan serves real Amigas too (--pair prints
    the code). HOSTMODE=LEGACY still works (run-host.sh --legacy: plain
    asb-chromed on 9223). If a Host already answers on 127.0.0.1:9220 (an
    installed one, or one the AmiStudio launcher started), AmiSurf uses it
    and neither it nor run-host.sh starts a second one.
  * Pairing sends a stable id for this Amiga (ENVARC:AmiStudio/ClientId,
    made once), so a Host 0.1.1+ replaces the Amiga's old token when it
    pairs again instead of keeping both.
  * AmiKit edition: Host/move-to-host.sh copies history, bookmarks,
    settings, passwords and the Chromium profile to the standalone
    AmiStudio Host 0.1.2+ (~/.local/share/amistudio-host/browser), and
    the pairing tokens to ~/.config/amistudio-host/tokens, only into empty
    folders / when no tokens file exists, never overwriting. The autostart command now passes
    --background (needed by Host 0.1.2's run-host.sh; see HOSTCMD in
    Host/README-AMIKIT.txt).
  * The AmiKit edition no longer switches to HOSTMODE=LEGACY by itself.
0.82 (17.10.2026)
  * AmiStudio Browser is now AmiSurf, an app of the AmiStudio suite.
    Programs AmiSurf, AmiSurf-020, AmiSurf-gadtools; drawer and archive
    AmiSurf (AmiSurf-0.82.lha); log PROGDIR:AmiSurf.log; prefs
    ENVARC:AmiSurf.prefs and AmiSurf.window. The old AmiStudioBrowser.prefs
    and .window are read until Settings are saved again; the engine keeps
    using ~/.config/amistudio-browser when ~/.config/amisurf does not exist.
  * New: AmiSurf talks to the AmiStudio Host (TCP 9220, service "browser"),
    which starts and supervises the browser engine on the PC. It finds the
    Host via HOST=, ENV:AmiStudio/Host, 127.0.0.1:9220, then a LAN search.
    An Amiga on the network pairs once ("Pair with Host": the code from
    amistudio-hostd pair; token in ENVARC:AmiStudio/HostToken, shared with
    the AmiStudio launcher). Clear requesters for not paired, no browser
    service, version too old/new, busy, refused, and no Host found.
  * HOSTMODE=LEGACY keeps the 0.81 way (plain asb-chromed on 9223,
    AUTOHOST). The AmiKit edition (Host/run-host.sh in the drawer) switches
    to it by itself.
  * The separate Linux engine package (asb-chromed-<ver>-linux-x86_64) is
    retired: install the AmiStudio Host instead (see ReadMe).
  * Includes the 0.81 fixes below (0.81 was not released on its own).
0.81 (not released separately; in 0.82)
  * Fix: the Home button opened the DuckDuckGo search page. It now opens the
    home page set in the host settings (menu: Host settings). A home page
    typed into the Amiga Settings window still wins; leave that field empty
    to use the host's. A saved HOME= that is just the old default counts as
    empty.
  * Host settings page: Save now says "Saved" (or why it could not save) and
    the address no longer keeps the one-time ?save=... link.
  * Fix: the splash screen was black with the MUI version on 16/24/32-bit RTG
    Workbench screens (it was drawn before RTG had been detected), and the
    main window then covered it. It now shows the picture, stays in front
    until it closes, and its status text uses a light pen.
  * Fix: changing the host or port in Settings could freeze the window: the
    connect and the ASBC handshake had no time limit, so a host that did not
    answer, or a port where something other than the engine listens, hung
    the browser for good. Now connect is limited to 3 s and the handshake to
    4 s. After a change in Settings the browser reconnects from its main
    loop. While offline it retries without blocking the window, waiting 2,
    4, 8, 16, then 30 s between tries, and the status bar says why it
    failed (no answer, cannot connect, wrong port, token rejected).
  * A/V sync: the engine now measures how long a picture takes from Chrome to
    the Amiga screen and sends that number with its once-a-second stats. The
    client sizes its sound buffer to match: it drops or repeats one sample
    in 50-100 for small differences and jumps for large ones. 0.80 kept sound
    250-500 ms in the buffer whatever the picture did, and Paula's rounded
    rate slowly drained that buffer until a gap. The stats bar shows
    "av <video>/<audio> ms", and the log gets an "av:" line every 30 s
    while sound plays. This needs the 0.81 engine; with an older engine the
    client buffers as before.
    On a fast link the picture used to arrive long before the sound could
    get out of its buffers (sound ~200-300 ms late). While sound plays, the
    client now asks the engine to hold the picture back by the difference
    (only then: typing and scrolling on silent pages stay immediate).
    Measured in the emulator: sound within about +-100 ms of the picture
    (0.80: 200-400 ms late). AVSYNC=OFF turns the hold-back off; AVSYNC=<ms>
    shifts it (+ = picture later, - = picture earlier).
  * Host settings: a home page typed without https:// (example.org) is saved
    as https://example.org.
  * The About requester pauses the sound stream while it is open (nothing
    reads the connection meanwhile).
  * Log: breadcrumbs for the About requester, the Settings window, splash,
    connects (with how long a failed one took).
0.80 (17.10.2026)
  * New: DRM video (Netflix, Spotify, Prime Video, Disney+ ...) can work in
    the AmiKit edition once Google's Widevine CDM is added. The CDM is
    Google's and may not be redistributed, so it is not included: run
    Host/get-widevine.sh once on Linux. It copies the CDM from Google Chrome
    if installed, or (after asking) downloads Google's Chrome package from
    dl.google.com and takes only its WidevineCdm folder (Host/data/
    WidevineCdm). install-amikit.sh offers this too (--widevine,
    --no-widevine). With Google Chrome installed the engine finds Chrome's
    CDM by itself. Without a CDM, Netflix shows error M7701-1003.
  * The engine (asb-chromed) points Chromium at that CDM at every start and
    says in host.log which one it uses, or how to add one.
  * Linux browsers only get Widevine L3, so the services limit quality
    (Netflix up to 1080p, often 720p; Disney+ 480p-720p; Prime Video SD).
    Tested with a public Widevine test stream (picture and sound reach the
    Amiga); Netflix itself not tested yet.
0.79 (17.10.2026)
  * Fix: the main menu (the "hamburger" button at the right of the toolbar)
    flickered and could crash the machine (seen on Kickstart 3.2, MUI 5,
    24-bit RTG Workbench). With MUI 5 it is now MUI's own popup menu, drawn
    the same way as MUI 5 draws the menu strip, and the page is not redrawn
    while it is open. Choose an entry with the mouse or the keyboard; Esc or
    a click outside closes it. Shortcuts are shown on the right.
  * The menu opens once MUI has finished with the button click (it used to
    open while MUI was still switching the active object).
  * Older MUI and the GadTools version keep the menu in its own window; there,
    too, the page is no longer drawn while the menu is open (repainted once
    when it closes).
  * Log: more breadcrumbs around menus (where the menu window opened, when it
    is covered or loses the focus, when MUI's popup menu closes).
0.78 (17.10.2026)
  * Fix: signing in to a personal @gmail.com account failed with "Couldn't
    sign you in - This browser or app may not be secure" (Google Workspace
    accounts worked). The engine runs Chromium headless (AmiKit edition),
    and headless Chromium announces itself as "HeadlessChrome" in its user
    agent. It now sends the user agent of a normal Chrome of the same
    version (HTTP header, navigator.userAgent, workers). Also removed: the
    navigator.webdriver JavaScript override (the browser flag already makes
    it a plain false; the override itself was detectable), Runtime.enable,
    and every script and binding the engine used to put into pages.
  * Fix: the "save the password" bar's Save / Never for this site / Not now
    buttons could not be clicked on some pages. They relied on the page
    passing the click on; a page that covers itself with an overlay or stops
    clicks swallowed them. The engine now checks the click position against
    the bar's buttons itself before it forwards the click.
  * Passwords: a login is offered for saving when you click a form's button
    or press Return in it (checked by the engine at that moment, no page
    script). The bar is shown again on following pages for 2 minutes only.
    "Never for this site" now also works on a site with a saved login.
0.77 (17.10.2026)
  * Fix: Backspace in the address bar deleted the whole address. A click into
    the address used to "select all" invisibly, so the next Backspace or Del
    wiped it. Now a click just places the cursor and Backspace, Ctrl-H, Del
    and the cursor keys edit one character (MUI and GadTools). A-L still
    selects the address so that typing replaces it; only after Ctrl-A do
    Backspace/Del clear it. Ctrl-H works as Backspace (MUI 5 passes no
    input event to edit hooks; Ctrl keys are now recognised there too).
  * Menus no longer strobe: the main menu (menu button) and the right-click menu are
    now real borderless windows on the screen, so page frames can never
    paint over them. They may extend past a small browser window (the whole
    28-item menu fits even on a 640x256 screen); cursor keys, Return and
    Esc work in them, clicking elsewhere closes them. If the window cannot
    be opened the menu is drawn in the page as before.
  * While an Intuition/MagicMenu menu strip is open on our window, page
    frames are held back and the page repainted once when it closes.
  * Hangs/crash hardening: on a slow Amiga a playing video kept the frame
    loop busy for many seconds so a menu click looked like a hang; the loop
    now yields to the mouse and keyboard every 100 ms. Page blits are
    clamped (no negative or out-of-page rectangles), menus are fitted to
    the window/screen at any size or position, MUI's "more input queued"
    case no longer sleeps, and the loop polls while a menu window is open.
  * New Amiga-side log PROGDIR:AmiStudioBrowser.log (LOG=<file>, LOG=OFF):
    startup info, connection, menus, errors, main-loop stalls and crash
    breadcrumbs, written line by line; the previous run is kept as .old.
  * AmiKit edition: the engine's data (history, bookmarks, settings,
    passwords, Chromium profile, host.log) now lives inside the drawer in
    AmiStudioBrowser/Host/data/ and is moved there from
    ~/.local/state/amistudio-browser-amikit/ on first start (a note is left
    in the old folder). Falls back to the old folder, with the reason in
    host.log, if the drawer is read-only, noexec or cannot hold symlinks.
    ASB_STATE= overrides. Downloads still go to ~/Downloads.
  * Credits brought up to date: the stale note about the removed classic
    engine is gone; OpenSSL, zlib and Zstandard (statically linked into the
    Linux engine) are credited with their licence texts; Lucide, stb_image
    and QOI are named in About.
0.76 (16.10.2026)
  * No more flicker over menus and the address list. Page frames used to be
    drawn over an open menu (main menu, right-click menu, address
    suggestions) and the menu drawn again on top, so on a playing video the
    menu flashed. Frames now skip the menu's rectangle.
  * Typing in the address bar no longer flashes the suggestion list (it was
    erased and repainted on every key) and no longer repaints the whole page
    when a list closes: only the uncovered strip is repainted.
  * Menus are drawn without first filling the whole box, so hovering does
    not flash.
0.75 (16.10.2026)
  * Fix: Esc quit the MUI browser in a Workbench window (MUI turns Esc into
    a close request, which was treated like the close gadget). Esc now goes
    to the page: it leaves video fullscreen and closes page dialogs (and
    stops a page that is still loading). Only the close gadget quits.
  * Fix: maximising a video (YouTube/HTML5 fullscreen button) showed it
    smaller instead of filling the view. Chrome dropped the host's viewport
    size in fullscreen and sent frames at the Linux screen's size; the host
    now emulates a screen the size of the Amiga view, puts the viewport
    back when fullscreen starts or ends and holds back wrong-size frames.
    Use F10 as well to fill the whole Amiga screen.
  * Frame guards: frames larger than 4096 pixels or with a bad pixel format
    are dropped, and the page area is cleared when the page gets smaller.
0.74 (16.10.2026)
  * Sound. The host runs the browser on a private PulseAudio/PipeWire null
    sink, captures it (parec, or ffmpeg) at 22050 Hz stereo and streams it
    in 40 ms ASBC AUDIO packets with a host timestamp; silence costs 10
    bytes a packet. The Amiga plays it through AHI (16-bit on the 040
    build, 8-bit on 020) or Paula via audio.device, double-buffered, with a
    ~250 ms jitter buffer that re-primes after an underrun and drops old
    audio past 500 ms so sound stays near the picture. Audio is serviced
    between network messages, so it keeps playing when frames are dropped.
  * Formats: 8-bit signed stereo (default), 16-bit (AHI on 040), IMA ADPCM
    (SOUND=ADPCM). The client lists what it can play in HELLO; old hosts
    and old clients just have no sound.
  * SOUND=ON|OFF|ADPCM tooltype, Amiga-M / menu mute (the host stops
    streaming while muted), audio stats in the STATS status bar.
  * Packages: AmiStudioBrowser-0.74-AmiKit.lha holds the Amiga drawer with
    the Linux engine in AmiStudioBrowser/Host, so one unpack on a drive
    Linux can see is a full AmiKit setup. The small Amiga-only LHA and the
    host tarball are still there.
0.73 (16.10.2026)
  * Clipboard keys in the address and find fields: Ctrl-C copies, Ctrl-X
    cuts, Ctrl-V pastes and Ctrl-A selects all, using the Amiga clipboard
    (clipboard.device unit 0, IFF FTXT). Before, Ctrl-C/Ctrl-X typed a box
    character. Right Amiga-C/X/V/A do the same in the GadTools build; MUI
    keeps its own Amiga-C/X/V. Control characters are never typed into a
    field, and pasted newlines/tabs become spaces.
  * On the page, Ctrl-C copies and Ctrl-X cuts the page's selection
    (including the selection inside a focused text box) to the Amiga
    clipboard, and Ctrl-A selects all. Needs the 0.73 host for cut and
    select all; an older host copies instead.
0.71 (16.10.2026)
  * Firefox backend: asb-chromed -browser firefox drives Firefox over
    WebDriver BiDi (Firefox's own protocol; no CDP). Chrome stays the
    default. Choose per client with the BROWSER=FIREFOX|CHROME tooltype or
    Settings > Browser; the client asks for it when it connects and the
    host restarts the other browser. See "Backends" in README for what
    each one can and cannot do.
  * Screen fix: the client's own screen now has exactly the mode's size.
    Before, a Workbench larger than the RTG mode gave a scrolling screen
    whose page was wider than what was shown (Google cut off on the right).
  * SCREENMODE=1280x800 (tooltype, prefs or argument) picks that RTG mode,
    or the closest one there is.
0.70 (16.10.2026)
  * MUI is now the main interface (AmiStudio suite build standard): MUI tab
    strip, icon toolbar, address field with padlock, find bar with match
    count, status bar, MUI menus (Project / Navigate / View) with Amiga-key
    shortcuts, MUI Settings window, "About AmiStudio Browser 0.70". The page,
    popup menu, right-click menu and address suggestions are shared with the
    GadTools client, which ships as AmiStudioBrowser-gadtools.
  * Three binaries: AmiStudioBrowser (68040/060 + FPU), AmiStudioBrowser-020
    (68020/030 + 68881/68882) and AmiStudioBrowser-gadtools. No soft-float
    build any more. Without an FPU a requester explains it and the program
    quits.
  * Release archive AmiStudioBrowser-0.70.lha (jlha, LHA level 1).
  * Fix: the popup menu dropped its last entry (Quit).
0.69 (16.10.2026)
  * Pre-converted frames: the client tells the host its screen format
    (Picasso96 RGB565/555 in either byte order, 24/32-bit, or AGA 8-bit with
    its palette). The host converts the changed areas, dithers onto the
    palette for AGA, and sends them raw or RLE-packed. The client copies them
    with p96WritePixelArray, WritePixelArray or WriteChunkyPixels, with no
    decoding. FRAMES=QOI keeps the old path.
  * Speed stats in the status bar: fps, KB/s, client ms per frame and host
    encode ms. Turn them on with the STATS tooltype or the menu.
  * Sends less: the client acknowledges each update and the host keeps at
    most 2 in flight, dropping frames in between. When the page scrolls, the
    host sends a scroll command; the client moves what it already shows and
    only the new strip is sent. Pages that don't change send nothing.
  * Benchmarks: bench/ (5 pages, 68040 RTG, 68020 AGA, Linux stream).
0.68 (16.10.2026)
  * Zoom (A-+/A--/A-0, menu, Ctrl+wheel on Linux) with the zoom % shown in the
    status bar. Bigger UI: UIFONT=, TOOLBAR=LARGE / BIGUI, SCREENMODE=AUTO.
  * Popup menu, right-click menu (open link in new tab, copy link, save
    image, back, reload), keyboard shortcuts that are the same on Amiga and
    Linux.
  * Address-bar suggestions from history and bookmarks; searches use the
    engine set in asb://settings.
  * Find with a match count and next/previous.
  * asb://downloads, asb://bookmarks (edit, delete, search), asb://history,
    asb://settings and asb://passwords pages (Host check + CSRF key).
  * Password saving: AES-256-GCM store on the host, offered after a login,
    filled in on the next visit.
  * Save page as PNG/PDF/HTML, print through printer.device, view source.
  * Tab drag to reorder, reopen closed tab, padlock for https, full screen
    (F10 Amiga / F11 Linux), Amiga settings window (ENV:/ENVARC: prefs).
  * Remote hosts: 5 failed handshakes in 60 s ban the IP for 5 minutes. A
    client replaced by another one no longer reconnects by itself.
  * Fixes: missing frames after a new client or a cross-process navigation;
    About closed at once when opened from the menu; offline reconnect under
    UAE.
  * Tests: make test-host, emu/test68.sh, emu/asbaga.fs-uae.in (AGA).
0.67 (16.10.2026)
  * Single 68020 client (no FPU needed, runs on any 020+); 040 build removed.
  * AmiStudio Paint-style UI: THEME=PRO dark theme, SCREENFONT=TOPAZ, flat
    24x24 icons loaded from PROGDIR:Icons/, status bar, RTG screen-mode picker
    (SCREENMODE, MODEREQ tooltypes, AGA fallback with dither), splash, About
    (Help key), DualPNG icons, DEBUG tooltype.
  * ASBC protocol v2 with optional shared token. asb-chromed -listen ADDR
    needs -token (8+ chars) for non-loopback addresses. CHROMEHOST may be a
    hostname; TOKEN tooltype/prefs.
  * Fixes: back/reload/tab messages dropped; back/forward; iframes clobbering
    the URL and loading state; new-tab selection; stray connections killing
    the real client; stale X socket; \u escapes; SDL text rendering; warnings.
  * SDL client: tab strip, Ctrl+F find, Ctrl+T, Ctrl+W.
  * Toolbar icons based on Lucide (ISC licence).

Unreleased (Chrome backend)
  * The browser is now a thin client for Google Chrome running on a Linux
    host: asb-chromed drives Chrome over the DevTools protocol and streams
    changed screen regions (QOI, PNG fallback) to AmiStudioBrowser over the
    ASBC protocol (port 9223). Input, navigation, tabs, find, clipboard,
    bookmarks/history and downloads (AmiKit:Downloads) go the other way.
  * The in-house engine (HTML/CSS/layout/paint, libcss, QuickJS-ng, MintVID
    video, AmiSSL) was removed from the main line. It is kept at git tag
    classic-final and branch classic.
  * asb and asb-mui are no longer shipped. AmiSSL and MUI are not needed.
0.66 (2026-10-15)
  * Real CSS in the style engine (libcss) instead of 0.64 workarounds:
    grid-template-columns/rows (px, %, fr, repeat(), auto-fill/auto-fit,
    minmax(), fit-content()), grid-column/grid-row/grid-area with line
    numbers, negative lines and span, auto placement; gap/row-gap/
    column-gap; aspect-ratio; border-radius (all corners, px or %);
    object-fit.
  * calc() works in every length property (width, margins, paddings,
    inset, gap, radius, flex-basis...), mixing px, em/rem, vw/vh and %.
  * CSS Color 4 (rgb(1 2 3 / 50%), #rrggbbaa, hsl/hwb, fractional
    values) and logical properties (margin-inline, padding-block, inset,
    inset-inline-start ...; assumes left-to-right horizontal text).
  * Removed hacks: Tailwind class-name sniffing (rounded-*, grid-cols-*),
    order/orphans/widows/column-count used to smuggle values, and the
    rgb() colour rewriter. Pages that relied on them now get the real
    property values.
  * The old built-in CSS engine (LIBCSS=0 builds) is deprecated: it does
    not know the new properties and fails 4 unit tests.
0.65 (2026-10-15)
  * Google search: typing in the Google home page box (Enter or the
    button), a /search?q= URL, or plain words in the address bar all
    search Google. Google now only serves results after its JavaScript
    "BotGuard" check sets an SG_SS cookie; the browser runs that check and
    sends the cookie (document.cookie now uses the real cookie jar), but
    Google may still refuse it. When Google answers with "enable
    JavaScript", "Update your browser" or its CAPTCHA page, the same query
    opens on Brave Search (plain HTML results, next page works) with a
    yellow banner and a "Try Google again" link.
  * Address bar: text that isn't a web address searches Google;
    "amiga.org" or "localhost:8080" still go straight to the site.
  * Loading feedback: busy pointer while a page loads, Reload turns into
    Stop, an animated gauge and "Loading <host>... / Running scripts n/N"
    in the status bar; Esc or Stop cancels.
  * Link hover: the pointer becomes a hand over links (OS 3.2 link pointer,
    built-in hand sprite on 3.0/3.1) and the link address shows in the
    status bar.
  * Fixes: a display:flex <textarea> (Google's search box) is a form
    field again; curly quotes no longer break scripts and attributes on
    the Amiga; pages that fade in with body{opacity:0} (Brave) show up.
0.64 (2026-10-14)
  * CSS Grid tracks (repeat(N) → equal columns), gap, object-fit
    cover/contain, overflow:hidden clipping, border-radius, and
    position:sticky. Tailwind rgb(r g b / a) colours now cascade.
  * Linux asb-sdl chrome is Chrome-like: tab, back/forward/reload,
    omnibox, scrollbar, find-in-page (Ctrl+F), hover URL, zoom,
    Alt+Left/Right, Ctrl+R / F5. Pages paint full-bleed.
  * YouTube on 68k: skip the ~11 MB Kevlar/Polymer hydrator and paint
    search results from ytInitialData. Job nodes use the task-safe
    allocator (UI + video process).
  * GCC 16 / AmigaDev: _sfdc_vararg for Roadshow, css_fixed_or_calc
    compound literals, int32_t for JS_ToInt32.
0.63 (2026-10-14)
  * New CSS engine: the cascade is now NetSurf's libcss (MIT). Complex
    selectors, media queries, !important and inheritance follow the
    standard, so GitHub, Hacker News, Wikipedia and Google home look much
    closer to a desktop browser. Wikipedia no longer turns dark.
  * Up to 32 style sheets per page (was 8).
  * Link numbers [n] are no longer drawn next to links (clutter).
  * Faster JavaScript on 68k: compiled scripts are cached in
    PROGDIR:JSCache; a much cheaper script-timeout clock; a faster memory
    allocator; JSON.parse about 20x faster. YouTube script time on a JIT
    68040 fell from about 72 s to 21-25 s.
  * Disk cache default raised to 48 MB, so YouTube's large style sheets
    stay cached (that was most of the "18 s CSS" delay).
  * Script time limits scale with the machine's speed; new JSPAGETIMEOUT
    pref (default 300 s). A stopped script no longer counts as an error.
  * Known: no CSS grid or border-radius yet; var() only for page-wide
    variables; YouTube can run out of memory on 128 MB machines.
0.62 (2026-10-14)
  * No more lockups while YouTube loads: the window redraws between scripts,
    the status bar shows "Running scripts n/N", and Stop or Esc cancels.
    The old page is cleared before the new page's scripts run.
  * YouTube search results lay out in two columns (thumbnail + details).
  * The browser now identifies as a current Chrome, in request headers and
    to scripts (navigator.userAgent/userAgentData). YouTube is the
    exception (it serves WebP-only thumbnails to Chrome).
    IDENTITY=AMISTUDIO switches back.
  * Google sign-in form now appears (first step towards Gmail).
  * Enter sends keydown/keypress/keyup to page scripts first.
  * document.styleSheets; longer JavaScript error stacks.
  * Runaway-script guard default is 60 s on Amiga (10 s on Linux): 10 s cut
    YouTube's renderer off on 68k.
  * Known: Google search returns a blank page (JS challenge / 429);
    DuckDuckGo shows a bot check; YouTube on a real-speed 68020 without
    JIT is not usable yet (TLS handshake fails, scripts far too slow).
0.61 (2026-10-14)
  * YouTube search results and watch pages now render: titles, channels,
    thumbnails, descriptions, related videos. Clicking a video opens the
    built-in player. (The logged-out home page only shows YouTube's own
    "Try searching to get started".)
  * DOM: real HTMLElement/HTML*Element interfaces with per-tag properties,
    native custom elements (define/whenDefined/upgrade, connected/
    disconnected/attributeChanged), MutationObserver with real records,
    persistent <template>.content, document.all, IntersectionObserver,
    plausible element sizes, anchor URL parts (pathname etc.), Audio().
  * Icon-only buttons use their aria-label/title as the label.
  * The runaway-script guard grows with script size (+1 s per 100 KB).
  * The JavaScript memory limit follows free Fast RAM (largest block minus
    12 MB, minimum 32 MB).
  * New UA=<string> argument/ToolType (-ua on Linux) sets the User-Agent.
    Default is now "Mozilla/5.0 (AmigaOS 3.x; m68k) AmiStudioBrowser/0.61".
  * Known: Google search answers "Update your browser" / enable-JS and
    DuckDuckGo a bot check, whatever the User-Agent. Pages are single
    column (no side-by-side flex layout yet).
0.6 (2026-10-14)
  * JavaScript, using the QuickJS-ng engine. Inline and external scripts,
    document.write, DOM (querySelector, createElement, innerHTML, classList,
    dataset, style, events, onclick, forms), timers, Promises, fetch() and
    XMLHttpRequest, localStorage, custom elements via the sites' own polyfills.
  * Runaway-script guard: a script or handler that runs longer than
    JSTIMEOUT seconds (default 10) is stopped and the page carries on.
  * Scripts can be switched off: NOSCRIPT or SCRIPTS=NO (argument or ToolType),
    saved as SCRIPTS= in ENVARC:AmiStudioBrowser.prefs. <noscript> content is
    shown when scripts are off.
  * Status line shows script count and time ("js 10/6 ext 5140ms").
  * Known: YouTube and Gmail load and run their scripts but do not render yet
    (see README). Next releases: 0.61, 0.62, ...
0.5 (2026-10-14)
  * Built-in video player based on MintVID (no GPL parts): direct media
    links, YouTube links, <video> and YouTube <iframe> embeds (poster +
    "Play video"). Three modes: Direct, Keyframes+audio (default on the
    68020 build), Download+convert. Audio through Paula. Frames drawn in
    full colour on RTG and dithered on AGA.
  * About window: banner, version/build, attributions. Project > About menu,
    About toolbar button, Help key, or about:.
  * ATTRIBUTIONS file and LICENSES drawer with the full licence texts.
  * Distributed as a plain drawer: copy it anywhere, no Install script.
  * Next releases: 0.6 (JavaScript), then 0.61, 0.62, ...
0.4 (2026-10-14)
  * MUI browser window: Back/Fwd/Reload/Stop/Home toolbar, URL field,
    status line + image progress gauge, persistent bookmarks and history
    windows. NOMUI argument keeps the old Intuition window.
  * All network traffic (bsdsocket + AmiSSL) and image decoding now run in a
    separate worker process; the Stop button interrupts a fetch.
  * Disk cache for stylesheets and images (PROGDIR:Cache or T:AmiStudioCache,
    CACHESIZE=<MB>, default 8). Second visit to Wikipedia: CSS 820 -> 80 ms,
    images 2.7 -> 0.4 s.
  * SCREEN=AGA: own 256-colour screen with dithered images for AGA machines.
  * Real position:fixed (stays put while the page scrolls).
0.3 (2026-10-14)
  * Main program is now the 68020 build without FPU: "AmiStudioBrowser".
    The 68040+FPU build is optional: "AmiStudioBrowser040".
  * Much faster layout: Wikipedia "Amiga" article laid out in 0.8 s instead of
    4.6 s (emulated 68040). Faster style matching (hashed keys + ancestor
    filter), exact per-element style cache, pre-split CSS declarations,
    page-lifetime memory arena, first screen painted before the full layout.
  * Off-screen images are only loaded and decoded when they come near the view.
  * CSS text colours and backgrounds are painted, including inline highlights
    and CSS custom properties (var(--x, fallback), :root).
  * Bigger default text (18 px), zoom with + / - / 0, FONTSIZE= and ZOOM=
    arguments and ToolTypes, saved in ENVARC:AmiStudioBrowser.prefs.
    Outline fonts are preferred.
  * Clear AmiSSL error messages (missing AmiSSL: assign, missing
    amissl_v*.library, old amisslmaster) and fallback to older AmiSSL 5.x APIs.
  * RTG: CyberGraphX-compatible screens supported as well as Picasso96;
    no third-party RTG headers bundled any more.
0.2
  * First Amiga graphical version: RastPort painting, RTG + AGA images,
    background image decoding process, URL bar, forms.
